Monk is one of the hardest classes to ascend. Perhaps you've been
playing
too many Monks, especially with that first comment of your's. There
are only
really 2 classes that can't wear much armor, and that is a Monk, and a
Wizard.
And wizards can do fine with daggers alone, and can sacrifice spell-
casting for
better AC and lvl 3 Cancelation from Mithril. Monks don't really have
that option.

Every other class, Barbarian, Caveman, Ranger, Tourist, Rogue,
Samurai, etc.
can wear any armor in the game.

My first ascension was a Ranger, because multishot enchanted arrows
do
even more damage than a Valkyrie with +7 Excalibur does, with the
added
bonus of poison on every shot. With the quest artifact, you get a
source of
near infinite arrows, which means near infinite ranged attacks. (An
average
of 2.5 poisoned +7 arrows hitting the target each turn beats any
artifact weapon
for damage. If the Ranger gets racial bonus for shooting, that goes up
to
3 arrows on average per turn.)

Lets see... 3.5 avg damage per arrow, with +7 bonus on each one makes
10.5
on average. Poison adds d6 damage (avg 3.5), and Expert skill would
add
2 more per arrow, so thats 16 damage + chance of instant kill per
arrow. From
a distance.

That is 2.5 * 16 == 40 average damage per turn, with an average of 2.5
chances
of poison-instant-death for an Expert end-game Ranger :-) Early game,
with
+0 per arrow and no poison you only got something like 14 or 15 damage
on
average with expert Bows. Only 5.25 damage with basic bows.

(Original idea taken from Jove here
http://groups.google.com/group/rec.games.roguelike.nethack/msg/abf1d0d1f0578218
)

Daggers are great for Valks, but even better for Rangers. Daggers work
the same
way as above for Rangers, except they get a strength bonus added with
them, but
lose their poison status. So we're talking +6 per dagger with 18/** or
25 strength.
Too bad that you don't get that +6 damage till near the endgame :-/
Not to forget Magicbane of course.

Additionally, one weapon of choice for Ranger is the Longbow of Diana
which
is a one-handed weapon that grants ESP when carried and Reflection
when
wielded. That means you can wear a shield on top of everything. With
possibility of skilled Divination, they can recieve the blessed book
of identify
from altar-camping.

Rangers start with Displacement and auto-searching. That means that in
an open room, enemies will usually totally miss you, even with a Wand
of
Death. (It has happened to me. Gnome + Wands of death hit enemies
instead of me because of displacement)

I'm not saying that Rangers are the easiest class, they are probably
not. (they don't
get as much HP as a Valk, that amazing late-game attack bonus is
well,
redundant when you ascend with the =oConflict. Still, its awesome to
take out
Death in one turn from multishot arrows :-) )

Anyway, my point is NOT that Rangers are easy (early game is a lot
of running, and using that 5 food rations you get to an advantage of
slower
play), but that they are certainly easier than Monks. Rangers are one
of
those classes who can poison their main weapon of choice, turning the
"The poison was deadly..." message into your friend.

